 "86ebceb9cf693c2c": {
  "container": null,
  "content": [
   {
    "citations": null,
    "text": "{\"attack_techniques\":[{\"id\":\"T1059.001\",\"name\":\"PowerShell\",\"tactics\":[\"Execution\"],\"why_relevant\":\"The alert shows powershell.exe being executed with command-line parameters to download and execute remote code, a primary mechanism for executing scripts and commands.\"},{\"id\":\"T1105\",\"name\":\"Ingress Tool Transfer\",\"tactics\":[\"Command and Control\"],\"why_relevant\":\"The command uses Net.WebClient.DownloadString() to retrieve a payload from a remote malicious server, exemplifying tool transfer over the network.\"},{\"id\":\"T1027\",\"name\":\"Obfuscated Files or Information\",\"tactics\":[\"Stealth\"],\"why_relevant\":\"The use of -NoProfile -WindowStyle Hidden flags and IEX (Invoke-Expression) demonstrates obfuscation and stealthy execution techniques to avoid detection.\"}],\"identity_signals\":null,\"key_findings\":\"An internal workstation executed a heavily obfuscated PowerShell command designed to download and execute remote code from a malicious external server. The command line contains multiple anti-analysis techniques (-NoProfile, -WindowStyle Hidden, IEX wrapper) indicating deliberate evasion intent. No identity anomalies were detected, but the malicious execution pattern on an internal host presents a confirmed threat.\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"severity_rationale\":\"This alert represents activity on an internal host with characteristics of active malware execution or compromise. The command demonstrates intent to retrieve and execute a remote payload with obfuscation, indicating an attack that has not been blocked by endpoint controls. While we lack confirmation of successful C2 communication or data exfiltration (which would elevate to critical), the execution of a malicious downloader on an internal system meets the threshold for high severity.\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"recommended_response\":\"Immediately isolate WORKSTATION-07 from the network to prevent potential payload execution and C2 communication. Conduct memory forensics and process analysis to determine if the download succeeded and what payload was retrieved. Review process execution logs on the host for any child processes spawned by powershell.exe. Investigate the user account domain\\\\jsmith for recent sign-in anomalies, privilege changes, or lateral movement activity. Query DNS and proxy logs for successful connections to malicious.com or similar indicators. If the download was successful, escalate to incident response for full post-compromise forensics.\",\"rounds_used\":0}",
    "type": "text"
   }
  ],
  "diagnostics": null,
